ne -6 -- scenarios.md","description":"Show who added recent scenarios"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01YU7Wppv1RvDvwKhzkB67KF","tool_input":{"command":"sed -i '' 's/IPaymentProvider.cs:55/IPaymentProvider.cs:53/; s/IPaymentProvider.cs:48 /IPaymentProvider.cs:47 /' tasks/pay-through-the-provider/judge.md && grep -n \"IPaymentProvider.cs:\" tasks/pay-through-the-provider/judge.md | cut -c1-80","description":"Correct two line references in judge file"}}],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":33,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The judge stage for `pay-through-the-provider` is done, and the verdict is **`story-conflict`**: the story now waits for your answer to one question.\n\n**Why it can't pass.** The code meets all three acceptance criteria, but one major problem is left that the build stage can't fix. The new browser test, \"A payment the provider authorizes leads on to the review\" (`tests/DcaShop.E2eTests/ProviderPaymentE2eTest.cs:23`), has no matching scenario in the shared specification's `scenarios.md`, which has no payment scenario at all. The sample's rule is that every new end-user test starts as a scenario there. `SharedScenariosTest` would fail on this test once it runs with `SpecificationPath`. You own the specification, so no stage may write the scenario. The Java sample has the same gap: no REST payment provider and no `order-summary-total` selector.\n\n**The question** is in `.agents/factory/decisions/pay-through-the-provider-02.md`, set to resume at the test stage:\n- **a (recommended):** you add the scenario, the test stage makes the test's title match it, and the Java side is recorded as open porting work.\n- **b:** you allow an exception that keeps this test .NET-only for now.\n- **c:** the happy path moves down to the integration level and the browser test goes. That contradicts the story and your answer to decision 01.\n\n**Two minor findings.** Neither blocks the story:\n- `RestPaymentProvider.CancelPaymentAsync` (line 83) answers \"refused\" even though no provider was asked.\n- `PaymentResult` (`IPaymentProvider.cs:47`) has a public constructor, so a code path could build a \"succeeded\" result with no payment reference.\n\n**Criteria and scope.** Each criterion is met by the code's behaviour, and each test checks every step of its scenario with the scenario's values. The slow-provider test also checks that the answer comes in under 4 seconds. Nothing from the story's out-of-scope list was built.\n\nAll four review perspectives ran through the reviewers the profile names. I changed no code; the only files written are `tasks/pay-through-the-provider/judge.md` and the decision record.","ttft_ms":5874,"type":"result","duration_ms":197978,"uuid":"f4f3af29-2b5b-484f-81d8-af74a5d7d914","ttft_stream_ms":4451,"time_to_request_ms":63,"first_content_frame_ms":4451,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
